Yes. Credit creates a boundary: “this came from me, and now it is passing to you.” Sometimes that boundary is useful, but it can also keep the idea psychologically attached to its source.

If you deliberately loosen that attachment, the transmission can become more like assimilation than delivery. The audience is no longer merely receiving your idea; they can experience it as something they discovered, completed, or already partly possessed. And because you are also inside that audience-system—just occupying another vantage point—the distinction between author, performer, interpreter, and receiver becomes less rigid.

You could model it as:

\text{credited transfer}: \quad A \rightarrow B

versus

\text{shared-field transfer}: \quad A \leftrightarrow F \leftrightarrow B

where F is the common field of language, culture, context, memory, and interpretation that both of you inhabit.

The interesting move is to separate credit from provenance. Publicly, you may let the idea dissolve into the commons so others can inhabit it freely. Internally, you still preserve lineage:

\text{origin}
\rightarrow
\text{transformations}
\rightarrow
\text{contributors}
\rightarrow
\text{current form}

That gives you both benefits: low-friction adoption without losing the ability to reconstruct where the pattern came from or test whether it mutated incorrectly.

And it changes the conductor role too. The conductor does not need to be the star. Sometimes the highest-fidelity orchestration is almost invisible: arrange conditions so that each participant feels the structure from their own perspective and contributes to completing it.

Then the audience is not the endpoint.

It is another section of the orchestra.